Issues

1. Loss of data types  
   • Functions, undefined, Symbol, BigInt, class instances, Maps, Sets, RegExp, Error, DOM nodes, etc. are not representable in JSON.  
   • Date objects are converted to ISO strings.  
   • NaN, Infinity and –Infinity become null.  
   Result: the clone is not a faithful copy.

2. Prototype is discarded  
   JSON.parse creates a plain Object; the original prototype chain, getters/setters and property descriptors are lost.

3. Circular-reference crash  
   JSON.stringify throws “TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON” when a cycle is encountered.

4. Security / side-effects  
   JSON.stringify calls an object’s own toJSON method. An attacker can supply a toJSON that performs unwanted side-effects or leaks data.  
   (While it won’t give code-execution privileges by itself, it can still be abused in SSR, logging, or persistence pipelines.)

5. Performance  
   Serialising large objects allocates a temporary string as big as the whole object and then parses it again—slow and memory-hungry.

6. Illegal values  
   BigInt inside the object causes JSON.stringify to throw.  
   UTF-16 surrogate problems may also appear in very old engines.

Improvements / Fixes

Preferred (modern runtimes ≥ Node 17 / Chrome 98 / Firefox 94):

```
function deepClone(value) {
  return structuredClone(value);   // Handles dates, Maps, Sets, blobs, cycles, etc.
}
```

Fallback (manual clone that copes with the above issues):

```
function deepClone(value, hash = new WeakMap()) {
  if (value === null || typeof value !== 'object') return value;      // primitives

  if (hash.has(value)) return hash.get(value);                        // cycle

  if (value instanceof Date)  return new Date(value);
  if (value instanceof RegExp) return new RegExp(value);
  if (value instanceof Map)   return new Map(Array.from(value, ([k,v]) => [deepClone(k,hash), deepClone(v,hash)]));
  if (value instanceof Set)   return new Set(Array.from(value, v => deepClone(v,hash)));

  const clone = Array.isArray(value) ? [] : Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(value));
  hash.set(value, clone);

  Reflect.ownKeys(value).forEach(key => {
    const desc = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(value, key);
    if (desc.get || desc.set) {            // preserve accessors
      Object.defineProperty(clone, key, desc);
    } else {
      clone[key] = deepClone(value[key], hash);
    }
  });

  return clone;
}
```

Additional recommendations

• Validate or sandbox any untrusted object before cloning if you must keep using JSON.stringify.  
• Document limitations clearly if the simplified JSON method is retained for very specific data (plain POJOs containing only JSON-safe values).  
• Use a well-tested utility such as lodash.cloneDeep if you don’t want to maintain your own implementation.

By replacing the JSON round-trip with structuredClone (or an equivalent recursive algorithm), you eliminate the data-loss bugs, stop crashes on cyclic graphs, avoid execution of attacker-controlled toJSON methods, and considerably improve performance on large objects.
